## 5. Key Design Decisions

**Idempotency (worker).** Unique constraint on `orders.order_id`. Before decrementing inventory, check whether a row for this `order_id` already exists in a terminal state — if so, it's a redelivered message; skip the work and just commit the Kafka offset. Do the "check + decrement + write" as a single DB transaction, so two redeliveries of the same message can't race each other either.

**Atomic inventory deduction.** One conditional update — decrement `available_tickets` only `WHERE available_tickets > 0`, and check whether a row was actually affected — rather than a separate `SELECT` followed by an `UPDATE`. This conditional update is what actually prevents overselling. Kafka's ordering guarantees are defense in depth on top of it, not a substitute for it.

**Partitioning by `event_id`.** Kafka guarantees same-key messages land in the same partition, and a partition is read by only one consumer in a group at a time — so every request for a given event processes strictly in arrival order, on exactly one worker instance. Different events, different keys, can process in parallel on different partitions. This is *why* you don't need distributed locks anywhere in this system.

**At-least-once + manual offset commits.** Commit Kafka offsets only after the Postgres write succeeds — never on message receipt. A crash between "received" and "wrote" causes a redelivery on restart, which is exactly why the idempotency check exists. Don't chase exactly-once semantics from Kafka configuration; get it from idempotent writes instead — simpler and more robust.

**HPA metric: CPU, on the Order API only.** CPU-based scaling is the right fit for a stateless, request-driven web service, which is what App A is. Don't put HPA on the Inventory Worker — its entire job is running at a small, deliberately fixed pace, and autoscaling it undermines the exact property the project is demonstrating.

**Infra via Helm, apps via hand-written manifests.** Use existing charts/operators for Postgres and Kafka — there's no learning value in hand-rolling a Kafka StatefulSet, and it invites bugs unrelated to what this project is actually about. Write the Deployment/Service/HPA manifests for your own two apps by hand; that's where the learning is.

---

## 6. Build Order

### Phase 0 — Planning & Data Contract
- [ ] Write out the exact fields on an order request: `event_id`, `user_id`, `quantity`
- [ ] Sketch the `events` table: `id`, `name`, `total_tickets`, `available_tickets`
- [ ] Sketch the `orders` table: `order_id` (UUID, unique), `event_id`, `user_id`, `quantity`, `status`, `reason` (nullable), `created_at`, `updated_at`
- [ ] Lock in tech choices from Section 3 — don't revisit mid-build
- [ ] Init the repo with the structure from Section 4

### Phase 1 — Local Infra Skeleton
- [ ] `docker-compose.yml`: Postgres + Kafka (KRaft, single broker) + a Kafka UI
- [ ] Bring the stack up, manually produce/consume a test message via the UI or CLI
- [ ] Confirm a Postgres client can connect
- [ ] No application code yet — prove the plumbing works before building on it

### Phase 2 — Database Layer
- [ ] Create `events` and `orders` tables
- [ ] Seed one test event with a small ticket count (e.g. 10 — small numbers make overselling bugs obvious)
- [ ] Write out the exact shape of the conditional-update statement you'll use for the atomic decrement — get the logic right on paper first

### Phase 3 — App A: Order API (happy path)
- [ ] FastAPI skeleton + health check
- [ ] `POST /orders`: validate input, generate `order_id`, publish to `order-requests` keyed by `event_id` — **no DB write**
- [ ] Return `202` with `{order_id, status: "PENDING"}`
- [ ] `GET /orders/{order_id}`: read-only lookup; no row found → `PENDING` (not a 404 — the worker just hasn't gotten there yet)
- [ ] Manually test with curl/Postman; confirm messages land in the Kafka UI

### Phase 4 — App B: Inventory Worker
- [ ] Kafka consumer skeleton on `order-requests`, manual offset commits
- [ ] Idempotency check against `orders.order_id`
- [ ] Atomic conditional decrement of `available_tickets`
- [ ] Write `CONFIRMED` or `REJECTED` (with `reason: "sold_out"`) to the order row
- [ ] Commit the offset only after the DB write succeeds
- [ ] Add an `order-requests-dlq` topic for messages that fail repeatedly, so one bad message can't wedge the consumer
- [ ] Run both services via Compose; fire manual test orders; watch status transitions

### Phase 5 — Prove Correctness Locally
- [ ] Set the test event to a small ticket count
- [ ] Fire more concurrent requests than available tickets (a quick script is fine — doesn't need to be the real load-testing tool yet)
- [ ] Confirm **exactly** the right number end up `CONFIRMED`, the rest `REJECTED` — zero overselling, zero underselling
- [ ] Kill the worker mid-run once, restart it, confirm redelivered messages don't double-decrement
- [ ] Don't move on until this is boring and reliable — everything after this is scaling/ops, not correctness

### Phase 6 — Containerization
- [ ] Dockerfile for Order API (multi-stage, slim base)
- [ ] Dockerfile for Inventory Worker
- [ ] Swap Compose to run the built images; re-run Phase 5's test against them

### Phase 7 — Kubernetes Baseline
- [ ] Spin up a local cluster (`kind` or `minikube`)
- [ ] Install Postgres and Kafka via Helm
- [ ] Write Deployment + Service manifests for both apps; ConfigMap/Secret for connection strings
- [ ] Deploy, port-forward, re-run Phase 5's correctness test against the in-cluster stack

### Phase 8 — The HPA Flex
- [ ] Add `resources.requests`/`limits` to the Order API pod spec — HPA needs this to compute utilization
- [ ] Install `metrics-server` if the cluster doesn't already have it (often missing by default on `kind`/`minikube`)
- [ ] Write the `HorizontalPodAutoscaler`: `minReplicas: 2`, `maxReplicas: 20`, target CPU ~50–60%
- [ ] Generate enough load to cross the threshold; watch `kubectl get hpa -w` and `kubectl get pods -w` scale out live
- [ ] Confirm Postgres connections/CPU stay flat the whole time — this is the actual payoff of the project

### Phase 9 — Observability
- [ ] Install `kube-prometheus-stack`, or keep it lightweight with `kubectl top pods` + the Kafka UI's consumer-lag view
- [ ] Build one view with three things overlaid: Order API pod count, Kafka consumer lag, Postgres CPU/connections
- [ ] Those three lines together are the entire story of the project in one picture

### Phase 10 — Load Testing & Results
- [ ] Write a Locust scenario ramping from 0 to a few thousand simulated users over a couple of minutes
- [ ] Run it against the full K8s deployment; capture the Phase 9 dashboard during the run
- [ ] Write up results (a `RESULTS.md`, or a section at the bottom of this README): pods scaled X→Y, DB stayed under Z% CPU, N orders processed with zero overselling
- [ ] This writeup is what turns "I built a thing" into "I proved a thing"

---

## 7. Success Criteria

- [ ] Under load exceeding available inventory, exactly the right number of orders are `CONFIRMED` — never more
- [ ] Every order reaches a terminal state; nothing stays `PENDING` forever
- [ ] A killed-and-restarted worker never double-processes an order
- [ ] Postgres CPU/connections stay roughly flat regardless of Order API pod count
- [ ] HPA visibly scales Order API 2→20 under load and back down after
- [ ] You have a dashboard or screenshot showing all of the above at once

---

## 8. Stretch Goals

Roughly in order of value for effort:

- [ ] **CI/CD pipeline** — reuse the GitHub Actions pattern from the Legal Document Analyzer project: build/push images on merge, optionally auto-apply K8s manifests
- [ ] **Deploy to a real cluster (EKS)** — a natural next step since you're already working in AWS for that project's deployment; reuses familiar territory instead of learning a second cloud
- [ ] **KEDA on the Inventory Worker** — scale worker replicas (up to partition count) based on Kafka consumer lag instead of leaving it fixed; a more "correct" scaling signal for a queue consumer than CPU would be
- [ ] **Client-provided idempotency keys** — an `Idempotency-Key` header so an accidental double-click doesn't create two separate orders in the first place
- [ ] **PgBouncer** in front of Postgres — matters more once you're running multiple worker or read replicas
- [ ] **Auth + per-user rate limiting** on the Order API (JWT + something like 1 request/event/user) to stop trivial bot abuse
- [ ] **WebSocket or SSE push** instead of status polling
- [ ] **Minimal frontend** — one page that POSTs an order and shows a spinner → confirmed/sold-out; mostly for demo purposes
- [ ] **Virtual waiting room** — the pattern real ticketing platforms use, admitting users into the "can even try to buy" pool at a controlled rate. Meaningfully bigger scope — only worth it once the core project is solid